# Volume 8 — Temporal Architecture

**Status:** normative computational design theory  
**Physical longevity and sensory evolution:** `NOT TESTED`

## Construct

Temporal architecture is the ordered behavior of a perfume through time. It
includes states, transitions, recurrences, adaptation, decay, and identity
retention. Duration alone is not temporal depth. A chord can persist for twelve
hours while remaining perceptually static; another can live for four hours yet
pass through several coherent transformations.

A **state** is a target-described configuration at a defined window: not just
“top,” “heart,” or “base,” but which recognizers lead, which anatomy is visible,
what texture dominates, where contrast lies, and what identity must remain. A
**transition** explains how one state becomes another. A **handoff** specifies
outgoing and incoming cues and an overlap. **Recurrence** means a cue recedes
and later returns or is echoed. **Metamorphosis** changes the object's apparent
material or emotional condition while preserving an invariant. **Decay** is
the loss of signal; it is not automatically an artistic transition.

Temporal architecture has at least three interacting clocks. The **physical
clock** reflects evaporation, partitioning, permeation, matrix, application,
and environment. The **receptor and neural clock** reflects adaptation,
suppression, sniffing, and changing sensitivity. The **cognitive clock**
reflects recognition, expectation, memory, and attention. A formula simulation
mostly addresses the first and approximates it imperfectly. Sensory testing is
required to observe the combined result.

The doctrine uses explicit windows appropriate to the perfume. A common set is
application, five minutes, thirty minutes, two hours, four hours, eight hours,
and twenty-four hours when practical. Very volatile or very persistent targets
may require denser or longer sampling. Window labels are not evidence; the
protocol must define substrate, dose, temperature, humidity, evaluator
distance, and whether the sample is re-sniffed or continuously monitored.

## Mechanisms

**Differential volatility** changes component ratios in headspace. **Matrix
retention** and **skin permeation** alter apparent release. **Suppression and
adaptation** can make a component emerge without a large physical increase.
Laing's temporal-processing work indicates that faster components can suppress
slower ones and that timing and concentration affect mixture perception.
Frank and colleagues showed that selective adaptation can reveal component
odors that were lost in mixtures.

**Identity relay** uses different recognizers to carry one object across time.
A citrus opening may relay rind identity into leaf, tea, wood, or blossom. A
flower may relay petal into wax, pollen, wood echo, or skin. A tobacco may relay
aromatic leaf into cured sweetness, wood box, smoke, and ash. The relay is
successful when observers can interpret the later state as the same named
object rather than a generic base.

**Temporal counterpoint** allows one facet to rise while another falls, creating
movement and renewed attention. **Delayed shadow** can deepen an initially
luminous object. **Late relief** can reduce sweet or dense fatigue. **Persistent
echo** can preserve character at low apparent intensity. These are hypotheses
that require exact time-resolved comparison.

## Material translation

High-volatility materials often shape entry but can also cause adaptation
holes or detached tops. Middle-volatility materials can bridge and carry
recognizers. Low-volatility materials can preserve texture and identity or can
become a generic persistent wall. Material note labels are less reliable than
actual physical data and mixture observations; some citrus-classified or
floral materials are unexpectedly persistent, while some nominal bases project
early.

Naturals contain constituents across volatility bands and can generate
intrinsic evolution, but lot composition and oxidation affect the result.
Composite-OAV models should preserve those constituent bands without replacing
the whole natural identity. Musks, woods, resins, sweet materials, and carriers
may extend a temporal field, but their presence does not prove coherent
evolution.

Stock dilution changes active mass and solvent fraction, both of which can
alter physical timing. A sub-ten-microlitre raw dose cannot be treated as a
precise temporal trace; it requires a measurable prepared dilution.

## Interactions

Temporal and configural effects are inseparable. As ratios change, the whole
odor object can cross a category boundary. A bridge effective at opening may
become a mask later. A shadow can be subordinate early and dominant after
faster anatomy leaves. Adaptation can produce apparent recurrence or reveal a
new facet, so physical concentration and perceived intensity should not be
equated.

Temporal evaluation also interacts with the measurement itself. Repeated
sniffing causes adaptation and can change the trajectory. Continuous TCATA or
TDS captures dynamics but increases attention demands. Fixed-window sampling
reduces continuous monitoring but may miss brief transitions. Method choice
must be predeclared and consistent across arms.

Hedonic trajectories can differ from sensory trajectories. Initial surprise
may fade, comfort may grow, and sweetness fatigue may accumulate. A single
final liking score cannot establish hedonic temporal depth.

## Failure modes

**Pyramid theater** assigns top, heart, and base labels from material
categories without observing states. **Persistence substitution** calls a
long-lasting base temporal complexity. **Detached opening** presents an
unrelated citrus or aldehydic flash. **Adaptation hole** loses the subject after
an intense opening. **Generic drydown** retains only musk, amber, sweetness, or
wood.

**Narrated evolution** describes transitions that the formula model does not
measure and the nose has not observed. **Window cherry-picking** selects only
times where the target appears. **Repeated-sniff confounding** compares arms
under different exposure. **Model promotion** turns simulated OAV decay into
skin longevity or sensory recurrence.

## Observables

At every window record named identity, required recognizers, subject hierarchy,
applicable facets, relative intensity, texture, contrast, spatial impression,
genericization, and liking. Record whether a change is emergence, disappearance,
handoff, recurrence, metamorphosis, or simple decay. Capture application
conditions and assessor exposure.

Computational diagnostics record active ppm, modeled vapor, OAV state,
distribution, dominant components, and missing data at each frame. Keep those
tables separate from sensory observations. Unknown OAV stays unknown rather
than zero.

## Controlled tests

Use formula-fixed temporal arms: complete architecture, target module omission,
and when relevant a ratio or substitution variant. Apply equal mass to the same
substrate under controlled conditions. Randomize codes and balance order.
Avoid evaluating too many samples in one session; adaptation and memory burden
can erase differences.

Use TCATA when multiple concurrent attributes matter. Use TDS when the sequence
of dominant attributes is the question. Use fixed-window scales for recognizer
intensity, identity, texture, and liking. Include repeated controls to estimate
within-assessor reliability. Analyze trajectories and state transitions rather
than only area under a curve.

For a claimed handoff, remove the bridge or incoming module and test whether
the transition becomes discontinuous. For recurrence, test whether the late
cue is recognizable and target-linked rather than merely persistent. For
performance, add separate distance or trail protocols; longevity on a blotter
does not establish skin or air behavior.

## Claim boundary

Temporal contracts and simulations establish testable hypotheses. They do not
establish physical longevity, sillage, adaptation, recurrence, or successful
evolution. Vapor pressure and OAV are model inputs, not sensory observations.
All performance and temporal-success claims remain `NOT TESTED` until exact
physical protocols are completed.

## Primary literature

- Laing DG et al. *Evidence for temporal processing of odor mixtures in
  humans.* Brain Research 651 (1994), 317–328.
  https://doi.org/10.1016/0006-8993(94)90712-9
- Frank ME et al. *Characteristic component odors emerge from mixtures after
  selective adaptation.* Brain Research Bulletin 72 (2007), 1–9.
  https://doi.org/10.1016/j.brainresbull.2006.12.010
- Pineau N et al. *Temporal Dominance of Sensations: construction of the TDS
  curves and comparison with time-intensity.* Food Quality and Preference 20
  (2009), 450–455. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.foodqual.2009.04.005
- Castura JC et al. *Temporal Check-All-That-Apply: a novel dynamic method for
  characterizing products.* Food Quality and Preference 47 (2016), 79–90.
  https://doi.org/10.1016/j.foodqual.2015.06.017
